  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Vedettes Îles d'Or & Le Corsaire link Le Lavandou (year-round) as well as Cavalaire-sur-Mer and La Croix-Valmer (in season) to Port-Cros, Porquerolles and Le Levant. It's the most practical option if you're based on this part of the Var coast rather than near Hyères.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the company's FAQ, leashed dogs are accepted free of charge on board, except on the Seascope (glass-bottom boat, too cramped), the Caps Sauvages cruise and the Naturalist Outing (too long at sea). On the islands, the company says dogs are allowed on Porquerolles and Le Levant except on the beaches, but prohibited on <strong>Port-Cros</strong> except in the village and on the path leading to the dam (islands map on its website). Also check the National Park's regulations before departure.</p>
      <p>The company's 2026 brochure specifies that dogs are accepted free of charge, kept on a leash and muzzled if necessary; the conditions of sale require a muzzle for category 1 and 2 dogs. Transport is free and the animal remains under its owner's responsibility. Your own ticket, however, is not free: expect €45 return for an adult to Porquerolles and €35 to Port-Cros from Le Lavandou (€33.50 and €26 for a child aged 4 to 11).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: for walking with your dog, Porquerolles offers far more freedom than Port-Cros; avoid themed excursions that exclude dogs.</div>

      <h2>On the islands with your dog</h2>
      <p>The Port-Cros and Porquerolles National Park reminds visitors that Port-Cros can only be visited on foot and that dogs are forbidden there outside the village, where a leashed dog is allowed between the Fort du Moulin and the statue of Saint-Joseph. On Porquerolles, dogs must be kept on a leash on roads and paths and are forbidden on the beaches. The Park also says there is no water point outside the villages: bring water for your dog. Camping and bivouacking are forbidden on both islands.</p>
      <p>The two sources do not fully agree on Port-Cros: the company mentions the village and the path to the dam, the Park mentions only the village. On excursions, the FAQ also excludes the Caps Sauvages, whereas the conditions of sale exclude only the Naturalist Outing and the Seascope: ask before booking.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The company has its ticket office at the Le Lavandou ferry terminal, opposite the tourist office, with ticket offices also in Cavalaire-sur-Mer and La Croix-Valmer in high season. Bicycles are accepted in limited numbers and for a fee, useful only on Porquerolles, where they are allowed. For a stay of three days or more, the company points to a car-keeping service in Bormes-les-Mimosas. Departures from Le Lavandou to Port-Cros and Le Levant take between 35 minutes and about 1 hour, and to Porquerolles between 45 minutes and 1 h 15 according to the ferryconnect booking site, which relies on the 2026 brochure. Timetables vary by season and the company may change or cancel them, notably because of swell: check the up-to-date brochure before leaving.</p>
    </div>

